            <ArticleTitle>Acute Knee Pain in a Child Due to Pigmented Villonodular Synovitis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>78-80</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.13107/jocr.2250-0685.316</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="INTRODUCTION" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Pigmented villonodular synovitis (PVNS) is a proliferative condition of the synovium, which is composed of nodules and/or villi and has an abundant number of hemosiderin-laden macrophages.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 10-year-old boy presented with an acute irritable knee. Emergency arthroscopy showed a nodular PVNS in the intercondylar notch. The symptoms resolved after resection of the lesion.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">PVNS of the knee in children is a rare entity. It can be one of the causes of acute irritable knee. Complete resection of the nodular PVNS can cure the disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
